1. Big Picture: Audio Path in Linux

To play audio from the 3.5mm jack, multiple layers must work together:

User App (aplay / mpv)
        ↓
ALSA (userspace library)
        ↓
ALSA Kernel Subsystem
        ↓
ASoC (ALSA System on Chip)
        ↓
Codec Driver (e.g., ES8326 / WM8960)
        ↓
I2S Controller Driver (JH7110)
        ↓
Hardware (DAC → 3.5mm Jack)

👉 This is called ASoC architecture (ALSA SoC layer)

🔧 2. Identify Your Hardware First (Critical)

VisionFive 2 (JH7110) boards typically use:

Audio interface: I2S
External codec: often
ES8326 OR
WM8960

👉 You MUST confirm your board’s codec from:

Device Tree (.dts)
Schematics
/proc/device-tree
📂 3. Check if Audio Drivers Are Already Loaded

Run:

aplay -l

Expected output:

card 0: ...

If nothing shows → audio driver not working.

Also check:

lsmod | grep snd

You should see modules like:

snd_soc_*
snd_pcm
snd_card
🧠 4. Device Tree (Most Important Part)

Audio won’t work unless DT is correct.

Look for nodes like:

&i2s0 {
    status = "okay";
};

sound {
    compatible = "simple-audio-card";
    simple-audio-card,name = "jh7110-audio";
    simple-audio-card,format = "i2s";

    simple-audio-card,cpu {
        sound-dai = <&i2s0>;
    };

    simple-audio-card,codec {
        sound-dai = <&codec>;
    };
};

👉 This connects:

CPU (I2S controller)
Codec chip
🎵 5. Minimal Working Flow
Step 1: Install ALSA utilities
apt install alsa-utils
Step 2: Test speaker
speaker-test -c 2
Step 3: Play a WAV file
aplay test.wav

👉 WAV is important — avoid MP3 initially

📁 6. Copy Audio File to Board

From PC:

scp test.wav root@<board-ip>:/root/
⚠️ 7. Common Issues (Very Important)
